@page "/expivot"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExPivot" Title="Overview"
          Lead="Excel's PivotTable for Blazor, drawn by ExGrid. Hand it your records and the fields they carry; your users build the report in the PivotTable Fields pane, and your application keeps the layout they made.">

    <DocsSection Title="Responsive sizing">
        <p>
            ExPivot draws its cells with ExGrid. A <code>ViewportWidth="ViewportSize.Stretch"</code>
            follows the available box while keeping column widths and the two-dimensional grid;
            a narrower box is reached by horizontal scrolling. Enclosing flex and grid items must
            permit shrinking with <code>min-width: 0</code>. A <code>MudExGridPaper</code> does so itself.
            See the <a href="exgrid#a-responsive-box">responsive grid Example</a> for both Chromes.
            This sizing support does not yet claim the complete mobile touch workflow.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A first pivot">
        <p>
            Declare each field once with <code>PivotFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</code>, hand ExPivot a source and
            a layout, and bind the layout. Under React the fields are <code>pivotRecordField</code>s over a
            <code>RecordPivotSource</code>, and the layout is held in state and set again from
            <code>onLayoutChanged</code>; the React Example pivots invented sales of its own, so its figures are
            not the Blazor Example's. Tick a field in the PivotTable Fields pane, drag it between
            Filters, Columns, Rows and Values, or open its menu with the ▾ beside it. The report is one
            ExGrid, with ExGrid's keys: drag across it, copy it into Excel, and press the <code>−</code> beside
            a region to collapse it.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstPivot)" Mud="typeof(FirstPivotMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A pivot drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExPivot runs on the same TypeScript core as React's, as ExGrid does: every Example on this site
            draws <code>ExPivot.Script.ExPivot</code>, and the Razor component <code>ExPivot.Components.ExPivot</code>
            stays in the package only until it is removed in this release. The report, the PivotTable Fields pane and the Pivot Toolbar run in the browser on both hosting models, so on
            Blazor Server a field dragged or a region collapsed no longer crosses the circuit. It takes the same
            <code>PivotSource</code> and <code>PivotLayout</code>. Your source still answers in .NET: each question the
            report asks crosses to it as PivotJson, it answers with the Leaf Aggregates, and the report and its totals
            are built in the browser from them, as from a server. A source that says it changed is asked again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCorePivot)" Mud="typeof(ScriptCorePivotMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Order the regions by market size: the regions follow an Order Key written in C#, largest market first,
            where they stood in alphabetical order.
        </DocsExample>
        <p>
            It carries <code>Layout</code>, <code>ShowFieldList</code>, the sizes, <code>Density</code>,
            <code>Culture</code>, <code>Caps</code>, <code>RedrawInterval</code>, <code>ChangeHighlightDuration</code>,
            <code>DetailsView</code>, <code>OnShowDetails</code>, <code>SelectionChanged</code>, which hands over the
            report grid's <code>GridSelection</code> as the pivot above does, the Selection Summary's
            <code>ShowSelectionSummary</code>, <code>SummaryFigures</code>, <code>SummaryFiguresChanged</code> and
            <code>OnSelectionSummaryChanged</code>, and <code>Label</code>. <code>Slicing</code> carries its
            <code>Budget</code>, read once as the pivot attaches; its clock and its yield are .NET's, and one that sets
            them is refused by name. A function cannot cross to the
            browser, so <code>Label</code> is asked for every id the pivot and its grid have words for each time the
            parameters are set, as the Razor component asks it on each render; the words cross only when they changed,
            and it answers from the id alone. A field's <code>OrderKey</code> is a function too: .NET calls it for
            each Item an answer names and sends the keys with the Items, and the browser orders the Items by them, so
            the Order Key you declared in C# orders the report here as it does above. A key crosses as a text, a number,
            a Boolean or a whole number that orders as its type does in .NET: integers, decimals, dates and times, a
            <code>Guid</code> and an enum keep their order exactly. Text keys are compared ordinally, as the browser
            compares every text key, where the pivot above compares them in the current culture. A key of any other
            type, two types in one field, and a function that throws are refused by name, field and Item included, when
            the report orders by them. Records double clicked while a
            removed <code>OnShowDetails</code> was still on its way to the browser open as if nobody had listened.
            <code>Chrome</code> takes a Pivot Chrome, and the parts it draws in Razor stand inside the pivot the
            browser draws: <code>MudPivotChrome</code> draws the Pivot Toolbar, the report filter band, the Layout menu, a
            placed field's menu, Filter…, Field Settings…, Value Field Settings…, the Stale Report's notice, the
            PivotTable Fields pane, the sheet tabs Show Details opens and its dialog with MudBlazor's components, and
            dresses the report grid as
            <code>MudGridChrome</code> dresses <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code>. A key typed as a menu opens is its
            first item's, on Blazor Server too, where the menu's Razor arrives a round trip later; a report with no
            values yet draws no grid to hold such a key in, and there it is not held yet. A panel's choices
            are native selects in MudBlazor's outline and its text fields are read in the browser as they are typed,
            so an OK pressed straight after a choice applies that choice on Blazor Server too. In the Fields pane a
            field's box, the Defer Layout Update box and a drag between Areas are the browser's, read where they
            happen, and a press or a drop made against Areas that have changed since they were drawn is refused, not
            applied to the entry now in its place. A Chrome of your
            own names its parts in <code>IPivotChrome.ScriptSlots</code> and marks its controls with
            <code>PivotScriptSlotAttributes</code>. Its <code>CellMetrics</code>, and inside
            <code>MudExGridPaper</code> the paper's Roboto widths and Density, size the report's grids and its label
            columns as on the pivot above. It takes no <code>ReportSource</code> and no
            <code>OrderKeyPolicies</code>: a server pivot answers Leaf Aggregates to the browser, through
            <code>PivotSource.Fetch</code>, and the report is built there, so a server neither computes the report nor
            orders it; give the field its Order Key in C# instead. It takes no <code>Clock</code> either: the browser
            paints the Change Highlight and writes a Stale Report's time by its own clock, in the user's time zone on
            both hosting models. The script it runs is bundled into the package, at an address that changes
            with its content; the grid it draws through, and the core under it, come from the Shared Script the ExGrid
            package ships, <code>_content/ExGrid/ex-grid-script.min.js</code>, which a page with a grid of ExGrid's
            beside it loads once for both. A Content Security Policy that lists the packages' files names it too.
            When the page holds another version's stylesheet, of ExPivot or of one the Chrome or its report grid's
            Chrome declares in <code>Stylesheets</code>, such as <code>MudPivotChrome</code>'s
            <code>mud-ex-pivot.css</code> and <code>mud-ex-grid.css</code>, this pivot says so in its notice as the pivot
            above does
            (<a href="getting-started#when-a-page-holds-another-version-s-files">When a page holds another version's files</a>),
            worded through <code>Label</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The parts of a pivot">
        <p>
            These pages and the API name the parts of a pivot as the list under the figure does. A field stands in
            one of four Areas of the PivotTable Fields pane: Filters, Columns, Rows or Values. A field in Filters
            is a report filter above the report. The Items of the fields in Rows and Columns label the report's
            rows and columns, and a column Item with columns under it stands over them as a Header Group. Each
            cell is one combination of Items. It is computed, as every subtotal and the grand total are, from the
            Leaf Aggregates: for each combination of Items that has records, the counts, sums and extremes that
            the Value Fields' Aggregations are computed from. For a screen reader the report's header rows come before
            its first row: a row for each tier of column Items, then the row of column headers.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="pivot-anatomy">
            2025's sales by region and country, with Europe open. A script draws the figure from
            <a href="figure/pivot-anatomy" target="_blank">the report on a page of its own</a>, where every part can be tried.
        </DocsFigure>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What ExPivot does, and what it leaves to you">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>It computes as Excel does.</strong> Items, subtotals and grand totals, the Compact,
                Outline and Tabular forms, Hidden Items, sorting by label or by value, Show Values As and
                Show Details follow Excel's PivotTable. The engine, <code>ExPivot.Engine</code>, has no UI,
                so a server can answer the same questions by the same rules.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It refuses rather than guesses.</strong> A value too wide for its column is painted
                <code>####</code>, never a shorter number. A copy carries the full-precision values, not the
                painted ones. Money held as <code>decimal</code> is summed exactly. A layout whose report would
                be too large to read is refused by name (<code>PivotCaps</code>), and the report on screen
                stays. A copy, or a paste, the report's grid refuses is announced as ExGrid announces it, in
                the words <a href="exgrid/clipboard#what-a-refusal-sounds-like">its Clipboard page</a> lists,
                which <code>Label</code> replaces by the same ids; <code>PivotWords.Japanese</code> words
                every one of them.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The page follows the report's Focus.</strong> The report is an ExGrid, so a move made
                with the keys — the arrows, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>, <kbd>PageUp</kbd> and
                <kbd>PageDown</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Tab</kbd> — brings the cell into the window when
                the report runs past the fold, as
                <a href="exgrid/selection#the-page-follows-the-focus">ExGrid's page follows it</a>; a page with a
                fixed bar sets <code>scroll-padding</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A report is read, not edited.</strong> Its cells take no typing and no paste, so the grid's
                validation is not offered on a pivot: the values are your data's, aggregated, and change where your
                data does.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Your application owns the layout.</strong> The <code>PivotLayout</code> is View State:
                an immutable record that says which fields stand where, with their settings, and holds no
                value. Bind it, store it as a user's setting with <code>PivotLayoutJson</code>, and hand it
                back next time.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It never holds the page.</strong> The answer, its cube and the report's layout are
                built in slices of about 30 ms, under a loading indication, with the report on screen kept
                until the new one is ready. A newer change cancels the work for an older one.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>It says when a script may read it.</strong> The grid a report is drawn with carries the
                <MudLink Href="exgrid/selection">Settled Mark</MudLink>, <code>data-ex-settled</code> on its root,
                once the keys and presses it has taken are handled and painted. Field List and settings drafts
                still take effect through their committing command. A report being rebuilt is said by
                its loading indication instead.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Installation">
        <p>
            <code>dotnet add package ExPivot --prerelease</code>, then place <code>&lt;ExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code>
            and <code>&lt;ExPivotStylesheet /&gt;</code> in your head, as <a href="getting-started#link-the-stylesheets">Installation</a>
            shows. For a MudBlazor application, add <code>ExPivot.MudBlazor</code> as well, place
            <code>&lt;MudExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;MudExPivotStylesheet /&gt;</code> after them, stand the pivot on a
            <code>MudExGridPaper</code> and pass <code>Chrome="MudPivotChrome.Default"</code>. The
            switch above each Example shows both: the behaviour is the same, only the drawing changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>In ExPivot for React</strong> (<code>@@ex-grid/pivot-react</code>, published with 0.3.0) the same
            seams are the <code>chrome</code> prop, one render function per part: a placed field's menu and the Layout
            menu (<code>menu</code>), Filter… (<code>itemFilter</code>), Field Settings… (<code>fieldSettings</code>) and
            Value Field Settings… (<code>valueFieldSettings</code>), and <code>grid</code>, ExGrid for React's own
            <code>chrome</code>, which dresses the report's grid and Show Details' grids. A menu spreads
            <code>usePivotMenu(context).item(index)</code> onto each command's control; a panel draws
            <code>usePivotPanel(context).view</code>, the draft the pivot holds, hands each choice to its setter on the
            context, and puts <code>first</code> on its first control, which takes the keyboard whenever the pivot asks.
            What a command does, what OK applies and why it is refused stay the pivot's, and a part not given is the
            built-in markup's.
        </p>
        <p>
            The parts that stand rather than open are slots too: the Field List (<code>fieldList</code>), the Pivot
            Toolbar (<code>toolbar</code>), the report filter band on its left (<code>reportFilters</code>) and the
            Stale Report's notice (<code>staleReport</code>). Each draws <code>usePivotPart(context)</code>, the view the pivot holds, and
            hands in its buttons and the positioned elements around them by <code>ref</code>:
            <code>context.control('layout')</code> for Layout ▾ and <code>context.anchor('layout')</code> around it,
            and so on. The pivot opens the Layout menu, Filter… and a field's menu in those anchors, and gives the
            keyboard back to the button that opened them, as it does with its own markup. The Field List's drag and drop
            is the browser's own: a field or an entry calls <code>context.startDrag</code> on <code>dragstart</code>, each
            Area and entry calls <code>dragOver</code>, <code>dragEnter</code> and <code>drop</code> with the index its
            view gives, and what a drop makes is the pivot's, as the built-in pane's is. Show Details' sheet tabs
            (<code>detailsTabs</code>) and what its dialog draws inside the pivot's modal frame
            (<code>detailsDialog</code>) are slots the same way: a tab's control is handed in by its view's
            <code>key</code> and pressed with <code>select</code> or <code>close</code>, and the dialog hands in
            Close and the element its records stand in (<code>context.anchor('records')</code>). The records' grid,
            the backdrop and Escape stay the pivot's.
        </p>
        <p>
            Whoever sets the font the report is drawn in owes its widths, as for the grid: <code>cellMetrics</code>, a
            <code>CellTextMetrics</code>, and <code>presentationDefaults</code>, a design-system Wrapper's
            <code>GridPresentationDefaults</code>, reach the report's grid and Show Details' grids, and its label
            columns are sized by the same widths. Handed again with the same widths in a new object, they ask the
            report nothing again.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>For a Mantine application</strong>, <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-mantine</code> draws every one of those
            parts in Mantine's components: <code>MantinePivot</code> takes ExPivot's props and stands the pivot in
            Mantine's Paper, its Field List, Pivot Toolbar, menus, panels, report filters, Stale Report notice and Show
            Details' tabs and dialog drawn by <code>mantinePivotChrome</code>, and its report grid's Chrome by
            <code>@@ex-grid/mantine</code>. Load <code>@@ex-grid/mantine/styles.css</code> and
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-mantine/styles.css</code> after Mantine's styles, under a <code>MantineProvider</code>;
            the pivot's colours then follow the theme and its scheme with no render. The Density picks the components'
            size, a slot the <code>chrome</code> prop names is the Consumer's over the Wrapper's, and what each part
            shows and does is still the pivot's.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>For a MUI application</strong>, <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-mui</code> does the same in MUI's components:
            <code>MuiPivot</code> stands the pivot in MUI's outlined Paper, its parts drawn by <code>muiPivotChrome</code>
            and its report grid's Chrome by <code>@@ex-grid/mui</code>. Load <code>@@ex-grid/mui/styles.css</code> and
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-mui/styles.css</code>, under a <code>ThemeProvider</code> whose theme was made with
            <code>createTheme(&#123; cssVariables: true &#125;)</code>: the stylesheets read the theme's colours as the
            variables MUI writes, so a theme without them is refused by name rather than drawn in colours that are not
            its own. A Compact or Excel Density draws the components <code>small</code> with dense menus, Standard and
            Comfortable at MUI's default size. The panels' selects are MUI's native ones, whose list is the browser's.
        </p>
        <p>
            On Blazor Server, input made in the report while the connection is lost never reaches it. Once the
            connection is back, the next input finds it missing: it is refused aloud and never replayed, and the new
            input is handled as usual, as on <MudLink Href="exgrid/selection">the grid</MudLink>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Saving the layout">
        <p>
            <code>PivotLayoutJson.Write</code> turns a layout into versioned JSON, and <code>Read</code> turns
            it back, refusing a version it does not know. A layout naming a field the source no longer
            offers is refused by name; call <code>PivotLayout.KeepingOnly</code> first when old saved views
            are expected. Change the report below, save it, change it again, and restore it.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(SavedLayout)" Mud="typeof(SavedLayoutMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What a selected cell stands for">
        <p>
            <code>SelectionChanged</code> raises the report's selection, and <code>ItemsOf</code> reads what each
            selected cell stands for: a <code>PivotCellItems</code> holding the Items its row's path names and, for a
            value cell, the Items its column's path names. A subtotal names only the fields above it, so it stands
            for every Item below; a grand total names none. When the selection reaches rows the report has not drawn
            yet, <code>ItemsOf</code> answers null rather than the part it could read. An
            <a href="exboard">ExBoard</a> link from a pivot narrows other views this way; its Example runs it.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Showcase">
        <p>
            <a href="showcase/sales" target="_blank">The sales analysis</a> is ExPivot in a whole screen: 60,000 invented
            sales by region and country across quarters, revenue with each market's share of its quarter through Show Values
            As, the PivotTable Fields pane beside it, and a few saved views to switch between. Collapse a region, drag a field,
            double-click a value for its records. Add <code>?chrome=mud</code> for MudBlazor's Chrome and
            <code>?scheme=dark</code> for the dark scheme.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

